Three changes from the last attempt. None of this has been built, so it is untested.

- **`is_space`** is unchanged. Its proof no longer uses `split`, which did nothing on that goal. A lemma `ite_step` now peels one `if c < k` at a time. Each branch that returns `false` is checked with `scalar_tac` against a small list of ranges that contain none of the line breaks.
- **`one_line`** now tests `out.len() < limit` before each push. Its loop then needs no invariant, only termination. The output is still "words joined by single spaces, first `limit` code points".
- **`truncate`** pushes only while `out.len() < limit`, and writes 0x20 for any whitespace it sees. That changes nothing for `one_line`'s output, which only ever contains 0x20 as whitespace. The no-line-break proof no longer depends on the shape of the indexing spec.
- **Assumptions I could not check:**
  - Argument order is `one_line_loop text limit out pending_space i` and `truncate_loop xs limit out i`.
  - The push spec gives `w.val = v.val ++ [x]`.

